This is the second of two pages in which Grothendieck sets up the combinatorics of stable curves, for the Teichmüller and Mumford–Deligne–Knudsen tower of moduli stacks \bar M_{g,ν}. The previous page defines a stable ν-pointed curve of genus g over k. It is proper, connected, of dimension 1, with only ordinary double points D. It satisfies χ(O_X)=1−g. S is a finite étale marking of rank ν inside the smooth locus. A component whose normalisation has genus 1 must meet D∪S. A component whose normalisation has genus 0 must have at least 3 special points on its normalisation.

This page abstracts that structure into a purely combinatorial datum Σ, which is the dual graph or 'combinatorial type'. Pass to the normalisation \tilde X → X over algebraically closed k. Let \tilde D be the preimage of the nodes and \tilde S ≅ S the marked points. Let Δ = π_0(\tilde X), the set of irreducible components of X, and abuse notation by letting D be the set of nodes.

Σ consists of the following maps:
- the inclusion \tilde D ↪ \tilde D ⊔ \tilde S;
- a surjection p: \tilde D ⊔ \tilde S → Δ, giving the half-edges and legs attached to vertices;
- a genus function g: Δ → N;
- a surjection q: \tilde D → D.

The axioms are:
- (a) the pushout D ⊔_{\tilde D} Δ is a single point, i.e. the graph is connected;
- (b) q has degree 2, i.e. each edge has exactly two half-edges (a crossed-out version said 'fibres of card ≤ 2');
- (c) Σ_δ (g(δ)−1) + card D = g−1, the arithmetic genus formula, where μ = card D;
- (d) card \tilde S = ν;
- (e) a genus-1 vertex has p^{-1}(δ) ≠ ∅. This excludes only the trivial case of X smooth of genus 1 with no marked points.
- (f) a genus-0 vertex has valence ν_δ = card p^{-1}(δ) ≥ 3. This is the stability condition.

He then defines the local dimension μ(δ,Σ) = μ(g_δ,ν_δ) = 3(g_δ−1)+ν_δ = dim M_{g_δ,ν_δ}, and μ(Σ) = Σ_δ μ(δ,Σ). Using Σ_δ ν_δ = card \tilde D + card \tilde S = 2μ+ν and (c), he obtains

μ(Σ) = 3Σ(g_δ−1) + 2μ + ν = 3(Σ(g_δ−1)+μ) + ν − μ = 3(g−1)+ν−μ = μ(g,ν) − μ.

So the stratum of \bar M_{g,ν} of combinatorial type Σ has dimension μ(g,ν) minus the number of nodes, i.e. codimension equal to the number of double points. This is the basis for the stratification of the boundary by dual graphs that Grothendieck uses in the Teichmüller-tower / 'Lego' picture.
